>
  <p>
    <strong>Territory running</strong> is a style of running game where the ground you cover becomes ground you
    <em>own</em>. Instead of a run being just a line on a map and a number for distance, you run a loop and the
    real-world area inside it becomes your territory — painted your colour on a live map of your city. Rivals
    can run over it to steal it back. Your daily run stops being a chore and becomes a move in an ongoing game.
  </p>

  <h2>How territory running works</h2>
  <p>The rules are simple, which is exactly why it’s addictive:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Run a loop</strong> that returns to where it started. The area enclosed is captured and becomes yours.</li>
    <li><strong>Claim more by running more</strong> — bigger and smarter loops take more ground.</li>
    <li><strong>Steal a rival’s turf</strong> by running a loop that overlaps theirs; the overlap flips to your colour.</li>
    <li><strong>Defend what’s yours</strong> — if you stop running, others will come and take it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The map becomes a living scoreboard. You can see your empire grow, watch a rival creep into your
    neighbourhood, and feel the very real urge to go reclaim a street before they lock it down.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it works when plain tracking doesn’t</h2>
  <p>
    Ordinary run trackers give weak feedback: a number that ticks up. Territory running adds the three things
    behavioural science says actually build a habit — a clear goal, visible progress, and something at stake.
    The combination is powerful. Losing turf you earned hurts (loss aversion), seeing your colour spread feels
    great (visible progress), and there’s always a reason to run <em>today</em> rather than someday (stakes).
  </p>

  <h2>Territory running vs other running games</h2>
  <p>
    Story-driven apps like Zombies, Run! immerse you in a narrative. Virtual-challenge apps like The Conqueror
    move you along a famous route. Territory running is different: it’s <strong>competitive and spatial</strong>
    — your real neighbourhood is the board, and other real runners are your opponents. It’s the closest thing
    to a multiplayer video game played with your legs on actual streets.
  </p>

  <h2>How to start</h2>
  <p>
    You don’t need to be fast. A 10-minute loop around your block claims your first territory; from there it’s
    about consistency and clever routes. <a href="/">Terrarun</a> is a territory running game live now on
    iOS — a GPS run tracker where every loop you run claims real area on a live 3D map of your city, rivals
    steal it back, and weekly leagues keep score. Pick <a href="/run/">your city</a> and you can be the first
    to claim it.
  </p>
